<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Definitions xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
	<CubeBlocks>
		
		<Definition>
			<Id>
				<TypeId>EnvironmentDefinition</TypeId>
				<SubtypeId>DefaultThermodynamicsLoop</SubtypeId>
			</Id>
			<ModExtensions>
				<Group Name="ThermalLoopProperties">
					<!--
						kg of coolant in each pipe block, so a ring's charge scales with its length.
						Per pipe rather than per loop: a fixed charge per loop divided the same fluid
						into smaller parcels as a ring grew, which made every parcel stiffer to
						integrate and made a longer ring couple harder to the grid while holding no
						more coolant. 50 kg matches the old 500 kg figure at a ten-pipe ring.
					-->
					<Decimal Name="CoolantMassPerPipe" Value ="50" />
					<!--
						W/(m2 K): how well heat crosses between the fluid and the wall it touches.
						Convective, so there is no thickness in it - the resistance is the boundary
						layer against the wall. It was a 0..1 quality against a reference
						conductivity divided by half a cell, which gave the game a second conduction
						pace and made the coefficient it implied five times larger on a small grid
						than on a large one, for the same fluid against the same wall. 160 is what
						the shipped large-grid loop was already running at; forced convection of a
						water-glycol mix is a few hundred to a few thousand, and a ring driven by
						one pump is a slow flow. See backlog.md, C20.
					-->
					<Decimal Name="HeatTransferCoefficient" Value="160" />
					<!--J/(kg K), real units: water-glycol coolant-->
					<Decimal Name="SpecificHeat" Value="3400" />
					<Decimal Name="PipeContactMultiplier" Value ="1" />
					<Decimal Name="SinkContactMultiplier" Value ="1" />
					<!--
						How fast the coolant moves with one pump at full power, metres per second —
						the same unit the terminal reports. This is how fast heat travels round the
						ring, and with it how long a radiator takes to hear about a reactor.

						Flow rises with the square root of combined pumping, so four pumps carry
						twice this, not four times.

						Split by grid size because it is a balance dial, not a physical constant: a
						small-grid pump is a much smaller machine driving a much shorter ring, and
						whether it should move its coolant as fast as a capital ship's is a
						judgement. Shipped equal, so the two grids behave alike until someone
						decides they should not.

						A small-grid pipe is a fifth as long, so the same speed is five times the
						parcel rate: the ring laps far more often and levels out sooner.
					-->
					<Decimal Name="LargeGridFlowRate" Value ="10" />
					<Decimal Name="SmallGridFlowRate" Value ="10" />
					<!--
						What survives with nothing circulating. A stagnant pipe still conducts into the
						coolant touching it; it just cannot carry that heat anywhere, which the
						advection rate above handles on its own.
					-->
					<Decimal Name="StagnantTransferFraction" Value ="1" />
				</Group>
			</ModExtensions>
		</Definition>

	</CubeBlocks>
</Definitions>
